This is not
necessarily the easiest

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:04.220
to understand but to say we put
it on reserve in the library.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:09.590
And with that, I
think, it's pretty much

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:12.260
the housekeeping item done.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:18.060
And what I'm going to do next
is to give you some slide.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.540
I'm not going to show
slides every week.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:24.280
But I'm going to
give you some slides

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:27.440
to see what's the
scope of this course

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:30.680
and some motivational slide.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:33.500
¿So any questions?

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.920
If you look at the title, this
is Nano-to-Macro Transport

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:37.500
Process.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:42.290
So we're emphazising nano,
we're emphasizing transport,

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:48.380
we're also emphasizing the
transition from nano to macro.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:52.850
So this is a slide from
a colleague of mine.

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:57.980
And here we're showing
the length scales--

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:02.570
that's in nature-- and length
scales for what we make,

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:04.820
artificially.

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:08.760
Later of course, we
have the biological side

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:13.100
you can see going
from molecules to DNA

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:16.760
to bacteria cells and
progressively increase

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:18.230
and then scale.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:24.230
And then if we look at what
people are making, for example,

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:26.900
these nanoparticles--
sometimes we

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:33.860
call them quantum dots as small
as a few, 1 to 5 nanometers

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:40.880
and to nanowires and
nanoelectronics, where this is

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:42.950
actually quite a few year old.

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:46.430
I think the gate length
in semiconductors

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:51.840
now is just around a few
tenths, 10, 20 nanometers.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.370
So those are the--

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:57.740
we're making smaller
and smaller devices.

00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:00.260
And of course at the end,
what we're interfacing

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:02.480
is at the macro scale.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:07.400
So when we go to small
scales, the questions,

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:12.020
because you have learned a
lot in the transport theory,

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:12.960
for example--

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:16.040
the question is are
they valid or not

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:20.570
and if they are not valid,
what are the alternatives.

00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:23.300
And also, we're
not just to looking

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:26.060
at the individual nanoparticles.

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:30.630
When you put them
together into macro scale,

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:32.220
what's the picture there?

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:36.140
So this is where this course
is trying to wrestle with.

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:37.320
And that's in fact--

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:41.720
most of my research is
looking into thinking

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:45.770
how we can look at the
small and then how using

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:47.930
the small to do the big things.

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:50.600
Particularly, most of
the work I'm working on

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:53.780
is heat transfer related
to energy and energy

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:58.590
as a large scale, so how I can
take small to go to the large.

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:04.430
And let me give you two
examples from research

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:07.430
to show that when
you go to nanoscale,

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:12.780
the classical theory that
you have learned breaks down.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:16.655
And the first example is
a heat conduction example.

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:24.210
We know in heat conduction
we have Fourier law.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:27.880
And we have thermal
conductivity of the materials.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:31.545
And this is one example of nano.

00:05:31.545 --> 00:05:34.590
This is in fact transmission
electron microscope

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:37.230
image of a layered structure.

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:39.390
One layer, the bright
layer is silicon.

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:41.520
The darker layer is germanium.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:44.580
So each layer is
about 2 nanometers.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:46.580
And then when you measure
thermal conductivity--

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:48.872
so you can take a silicon
because one layer is silicon.

00:05:48.872 --> 00:05:50.330
The other is germanium.

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:52.625
So you have started
heat transfer.

00:05:52.625 --> 00:05:54.860
It's parallel plates.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:57.410
And then you can do
thermal resistors

00:05:57.410 --> 00:06:01.310
to say what's the effect
with thermal conductivity.

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:05.210
And you can say if I
take a silicon germanium

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:08.060
thermal conductivity,
I will predict

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:11.910
that's the equivalent thermal
energy of those open circles.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:15.870
But the experimentally measured
dots are order of magnitude

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:17.140
smaller.

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:20.790
And in fact, we have to go
to complete different theory.

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:23.820
In this case, we have to
go to Boltzmann equation

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:25.530
to predict now--

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:28.620
See, this is a work that we
started in 90's, and now we're

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:32.130
doing quantum calculations
on those structures.

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:36.720
So there's significant departure
from the classical theory

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:39.120
when you go to nano scales.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:43.620
And as another
example, you learned

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:46.950
in thermal radiation,
[? black ?] body

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:51.180
is the maximum of
thermal radiation.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:53.580
But, see, say when
we go to nanoscale,

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:58.370
when we put the two objects
smaller, the distance-- this

00:06:58.370 --> 00:06:59.690
was on MIT web page.

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:00.723
I really like this.

00:07:00.723 --> 00:07:01.640
They're very creative.

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:02.600
What should I say?

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:05.300
This is better than what we did.

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:09.150
So that's Planck's law,
black body radiation.

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:11.720
And that's usually
considered maximum.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:17.510
And when we put two
surfaces down to nanoscale--

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:23.180
right here is one micron, 100
nanometer, and 10 nanometer.

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:25.160
We can go beyond
the Planck's law

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:27.880
by several order of magnitude.

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:30.730
When I say this is three
order of magnitude--

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:32.770
we're now at the four
order of magnitude.

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:37.510
And we're looking at what
happens between that one

00:07:37.510 --> 00:07:40.160
nanometer and contact.

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:43.790
And you say, why you cares?

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:48.770
The fact is when I put
many materials together,

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:50.870
they say it's
really particularly

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:51.770
when I'm doing nano.

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:54.500
It's really the
interface dominance.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:59.270
And so that interface
sometimes contact each other.

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:00.810
Sometimes they do not.

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:06.470
So this is the
[? connection. ?] I say,

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:09.980
how I can take this
nanoscale and eventually

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:12.140
go to macro scale?

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:14.870
So this is a propaganda.

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:15.515
I do nano.

00:08:15.515 --> 00:08:18.740
I say, once I was asked
by a graduate student,

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:20.270
summarize research
in three words.

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:23.820
That's what I came
up, nano heat, energy.

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:28.130
And what I had benefited
over the years is--

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:29.270
I'm a mechanical engineer.

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:30.850
I'm a mechanical engineer.

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:38.030
I always say at the end, I
do want to be useful, right.

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:43.150
So I think about atoms, but
I also think about continuum.

00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:49.690
And I say we have few
things that we can probably

00:08:49.690 --> 00:08:54.910
claim that we had really done
some change in the materials.

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:58.060
I gave an example on this
thermal conductivity,

00:08:58.060 --> 00:08:59.710
[? second ?] germanium
superlattice,

00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:01.180
that layer structure.

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:04.870
And we're taking that to make a
better thermal electric energy

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:07.810
converters because we need the
materials with the pool heat

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:11.090
conduction, and we still need
the electron goes through.

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:14.530
So this is a very
contradictory requirement.

00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:17.860
And currently we're
working on this photon side

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:21.070
that we're designing
nanostructure

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:24.270
using less material
to do solar cells.

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:30.840
And we have some of the
most conductive liquid

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:34.530
just by putting
nanoparticles in the liquid

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:36.990
and find out what's the
thermal conductivity.

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:41.100
In fact, at the end we say,
I don't want everything nano.

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:44.010
That's where you understand
if you understand physics--

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:46.950
now that's everything is nano.

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:50.850
So we work on people putting
nanoparticle into liquid.

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:53.160
Some say have high
thermal conductivity.

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:56.530
Others go to lab, they say,
no, we can't measure it.

00:09:56.530 --> 00:10:00.750
We struggled for four years
and eventually figured out--

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:05.310
we say, OK, eventually what we
need is macro in one direction

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:07.450
and nano in the other direction.

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:11.820
And that's how we achieve the
best thermal conductivity.

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:15.660
And we also have the
most conductive polymers.

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:19.620
I get a call every month
almost from an industry to say,

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:21.670
can we have it?

00:10:21.670 --> 00:10:27.000
So the question was polymers,
plastics, are typically

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:28.860
poor heat condition.

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:32.400
So people always want to
make them more conductive.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:36.642
Most work is making them
more electrically conductive.

00:10:36.642 --> 00:10:38.100
But, see, when we
ask the question,

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:40.050
I got a call from
Intel saying can you

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:43.690
think about how I can
better manage a hit.

00:10:43.690 --> 00:10:45.870
I started thinking
of plastics and how

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:48.020
I make them more conductive.

00:10:48.020 --> 00:10:50.990
And if you look at how
people usually do it,

00:10:50.990 --> 00:10:52.340
they'll put in a composite.

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:56.510
They'll put a lot of component
and make them average peak

00:10:56.510 --> 00:10:59.830
and higher heat conduction.

00:10:59.830 --> 00:11:02.630
And when I think about
this, OK, of course

00:11:02.630 --> 00:11:05.820
that's one of the approach.

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:10.290
But, see, the question we
ask is a molecule, a polymer,

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:13.740
really a poor conductor?

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:17.310
Is a molecular chain
a pool heat conductor?

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:23.190
If you think of a polyethylene,
which is 60% of the plastics.

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:27.390
And it's a carbon
per carbon bonding.

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:29.445
And carbon-carbon,
what's the highest

00:11:29.445 --> 00:11:31.910
of the thermal
conductivity material?

00:11:31.910 --> 00:11:32.942
Anyone know?

00:11:32.942 --> 00:11:33.650
STUDENT: Diamond.

00:11:33.650 --> 00:11:36.740
GANG CHEN: Diamond,
carbon-carbon bond.

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:40.730
So that suggests in the
molecular chain direction,

00:11:40.730 --> 00:11:43.813
I should have a high heat
thermal conductivity.

00:11:43.813 --> 00:11:44.980
So you think about the nano.

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:47.390
You think about molecules.

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:49.535
And in fact, I say,
of course, I can put

00:11:49.535 --> 00:11:51.380
that in more fancier theory.

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:53.975
I go to Fermi, and he
did work in the '50s.

00:11:53.975 --> 00:11:56.660
And he said
one-dimensional conductor

00:11:56.660 --> 00:11:59.210
has an infinite
thermal conductivity.

00:11:59.210 --> 00:12:02.400
So that gave me a lot of
motivation to look into that.

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:04.760
And in turn, we did succeed
in actually creating

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:06.530
the most conductive polymer.

00:12:06.530 --> 00:12:09.800
Except, it's in one
single fiber, nanofiber.

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:11.420
All right, so I say--

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:13.370
I got calls every
month from company.

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:17.670
I said, you guys have to
wait or you give me money.

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:21.920
We do more research because
I need to scale this up,

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:24.740
because you eventually are
not using single nanofiber

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:26.900
to put in your heat exchangers.

00:12:26.900 --> 00:12:29.360
So that's what I say.

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:30.740
You need to think of nano.

00:12:30.740 --> 00:12:31.670
You have a lot of.

00:12:31.670 --> 00:12:34.250
You can generate more
new ideas but also

00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:36.440
to think to how you
can go to macro.

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:46.130
So when I say, what's
nano for energy.

00:12:46.130 --> 00:12:49.610
If you think about
the energy, at the end

00:12:49.610 --> 00:12:53.495
it's molecules that mechanical
engineer are familiar with.

00:12:53.495 --> 00:12:58.550
It's the electrons, and
it's a photon, the light

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:01.442
or its lattice vibration phonon.

00:13:01.442 --> 00:13:02.900
Those are the basic
energy carrier.

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:06.860
There are some other terms.

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:11.570
But let's say those are
the basic energy carriers.

00:13:11.570 --> 00:13:15.950
What's their characteristic
length, the mean free pass

00:13:15.950 --> 00:13:18.290
and the wavelengths?

00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:20.180
So the mean free
pass of air molecule

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:22.130
will get into that very strong.

00:13:22.130 --> 00:13:25.390
In this room it's
about 100 nanometer.

00:13:25.390 --> 00:13:29.220
And that means they travel about
100 nanometer and the collide

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:31.820
with another molecule.

00:13:31.820 --> 00:13:36.470
And the wavelengths of
light, visible light.

00:13:36.470 --> 00:13:38.710
How much wavelengths?

00:13:38.710 --> 00:13:41.380
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:45.250
GANG CHEN: 400 nanometer
to 700 nanometer range,

00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:46.750
so half micron.

00:13:46.750 --> 00:13:52.640
And so a lot of this
in the nanometer range.

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:56.570
And if you think about
energy, fundamentally,

00:13:56.570 --> 00:14:00.460
if you can control the
energy level a material has--

00:14:00.460 --> 00:14:03.460
for example, if I make a
quantum dots that I show before,

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:09.040
5 nanometer spheres,
particles, what happens

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:14.240
is an electron, quantum
mechanics-- it's a wave.

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:17.270
And wave has a wavelength.

00:14:17.270 --> 00:14:23.780
And typical wavelengths is
let's say, 10 nanometer.

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:26.990
Now a wave of 10
nanometer, you try

00:14:26.990 --> 00:14:31.820
to fit into that 5 nanometer
quantum dot what happens.

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:35.050
You squeeze that wave.

00:14:35.050 --> 00:14:38.240
You squeeze that wave the
energy level will change.

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:42.656
So if you look at professor
Moungi Bawendi's home page,

00:14:42.656 --> 00:14:48.020
you will see the quantum
dots of different colors.

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:50.390
That's because the
energy level change

00:14:50.390 --> 00:14:54.030
when you make the size change.

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:58.610
So that's one way you
can control the energy.

00:14:58.610 --> 00:15:02.270
If you control the energy, now
come to mechanical engineering

00:15:02.270 --> 00:15:03.818
or any engineering.

00:15:03.818 --> 00:15:04.610
What are you doing?

00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:06.440
You control the thermodynamics.

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:12.640
Energy is the most fundamental.

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:15.560
So you can control
thermodynamics.

00:15:15.560 --> 00:15:17.930
And the other is,
let's say, transport.

00:15:17.930 --> 00:15:20.870
If you look at where
